      "## Cleaning-day blend ratios",
      "A few reliable combinations to start with, depending on the room and how bright you want the finish to feel.",
      "| Blend | Scents & ratio | Best for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kitchen Reset | Citrus Bloom (4 drops) + Pure Rain (2 drops) | Kitchens, after cooking and dishes |\n| Whole-Home Refresh | Pure Rain (3 drops) + Green Bamboo (3 drops) | Living rooms, open-plan spaces |\n| Laundry Day | Citrus Bloom (3 drops) + Cloud Cotton (3 drops) | Laundry rooms, linen closets |\n| Bathroom Brightener | Citrus Bloom (5 drops) + Pure Rain (1 drop) | Small bathrooms, powder rooms |",
      "As with any blend, start light and build up rather than the other way around — our guide on [how many drops of oil to use in a diffuser](/blog/how-many-drops-of-oil-in-a-diffuser) has good general starting points, and a cleaning day is actually a decent time to test a new ratio, since the room is already smelling neutral and you'll notice the blend clearly.",
      "## Timing it right: before, during, or after cleaning?",
      "The short version: scent after, ventilate during. While you're actively using cleaning products — especially anything with bleach, ammonia, or a strong chemical smell — the priority is airflow, not fragrance. Open windows, run exhaust fans, and let the space clear before you introduce a diffuser blend on top of it. Running a diffuser at the same time as strong chemical cleaning doesn't neutralize anything; it just adds a second scent layer to air you should be moving out of the room, not masking.",
      "Once the visible cleaning is done and the space has had a chance to air out — even fifteen or twenty minutes with a window cracked makes a real difference — that's the right moment to start your diffuser. This is also a good approach room by room: run your blend in the room you just finished while you move on to the next one, so each space gets its \"clean scent\" moment once it's actually ready for it, rather than trying to scent the whole house simultaneously while you're still mid-clean elsewhere.",
      "This sequencing matters more for some products than others. A spray-based mop solution or a bathroom disinfectant tends to clear the air faster than something like an oven cleaner or a heavy-duty degreaser, which can leave a noticeably stronger smell behind for longer. Use your judgment room by room rather than applying one blanket rule to the whole house — a kitchen that just had the oven cleaned deserves more airing-out time before you introduce a diffuser blend than a hallway that only got a quick dust and vacuum.",
      "## Room-by-room diffuser picks",
